Platform driver for the GS201 SoC of the Google Pixel 7

The driver 'gs201_platform' hands out the devices of the devices ROM
(board/google_pixel7/devices) to the other drivers. It switches the power
domains, clocks, and supplies that the devices name, the supplies via the
power-management firmware (ACPM).

It is part of every driver scenario of this repository, e.g.,
'run/exynos_fb_pixel'.

With the config attribute 'rtc', it also serves the read-only Rtc session for
the RTC of the S2MPG12 PMIC, which is behind the same firmware. The scenario
'run/rtc_pixel' reads the time:

! make run/rtc_pixel KERNEL=hw BOARD=google_pixel7

With the config attribute 'cpufreq', it sets the clocks of the three CPU
clusters, which are DVFS domains of the same firmware: cluster 2 = CPUs
0-3 (Cortex-A55, 300-1803 MHz), 3 = CPUs 4-5 (Cortex-A78, 400-2348 MHz),
4 = CPUs 6-7 (Cortex-X1, 500-2850 MHz). The steps come from the firmware's
frequency/voltage map. Requests arrive as ROM 'cpufreq_request'
('cluster' nodes with 'id' and 'khz'); the driver clamps them to the limits,
rounds up to a step, keeps cluster 2 at what the clocks of the others
require, and raises cluster 2 before and lowers it after the others. The
state is published as report 'cpufreq': per cluster 'id', 'khz',
'min_khz', 'max_khz', 'temp_c' and its 'step' nodes, per CPU 'xpos',
'khz', 'cluster', 'temp_c'. The temperatures of the three thermal zones
are read every 'thermal_period_ms' (250); 'cpufreq_stats' logs the
requests, rate changes and the time spent in them every 10 s. Without
requests, the clocks
stay as the boot loader left them. The policy, e.g. cpu_placer of
genode-muesli, is another component.

Host tests of the request planning and of the step tables:

! make -C repos/pixel/src/driver/platform/gs201/test
